# Odds, risks and hazards
subtitle: "How risk, odds and hazard ratios differ, when they agree, and why twice the odds is rarely twice the risk, worked through a stroke trial, a lung-cancer case-control study and survival curves."
tags: ["statistics","risk-odds-hazard","clinical trials","epidemiology","study design"]
teaches: ["concepts/risk-odds-hazard-ratios","concepts/baseline-risk","methods/case-control-studies","methods/interpreting-effect-sizes"]
thumb: "band \"Twice the odds is not twice the risk\" card blue seal"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical","take":"lines"}

chapter: Risks

## Risk and the 2×2 table
level: advanced
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: Group
    col_label: Outcome at 1 year
    row_values:
      - 'Drug: 100'
      - 'Placebo: 100'
    col_values:
      - Stroke
      - No stroke
    cells:
      - ['10', '90']
      - ['20', '80']
    cell_notes:
      - [a, b]
      - [c, d]
sources:
    - id: laupacis
      title: An assessment of clinically useful measures of the consequences of treatment
      authors: Laupacis A, Sackett DL, Roberts RS
      year: 1988
      finding: Proposed the number needed to treat, 1/(absolute risk reduction), as a clinically useful measure.
      doi: 10.1056/NEJM198806303182605

A paper can report a treatment's effect as a risk ratio, an odds ratio or a hazard ratio. They sound alike, but they answer different questions, and the gap between them can be large.
    draw grid row_title col_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1

All of them start from the same place, a two-by-two table of exposure against outcome.
    write "Every measure starts from a **2×2 table**: exposure by outcome."

Take a made-up trial. A hundred patients get the drug, and ten of them have a stroke within a year.
    draw cell_0_0 cell_0_1

Another hundred get placebo, and twenty of them have a stroke.
    draw cell_1_0 cell_1_1

    write id risk text "**Risk** $= \\dfrac{\\text{events}}{\\text{people at risk at the start}}$"

The plainest measure is the risk, the share of the people at the start who have the event.
    highlight cell_0_0 cell_1_0

    write "Drug: $\\dfrac{a}{a+b} = 0.10$ · Placebo: $\\dfrac{c}{c+d} = 0.20$"

So the risk is ten percent on the drug, and twenty percent on placebo.
    text rHead "Risk" #8f887c x 930 y 598
    text r1 "10 %" #2f6b8f x 930 y 461
    text r2 "20 %" #b5482e x 930 y 280

    write "*A risk needs a time window: here, one year.*"

A risk without a time window means nothing, so we say one-year risk.
    point at.ref col_title gesture underline

    write id rd text "**Risk difference** $RD = 0.10 - 0.20 = -0.10$"

Subtract one risk from the other, and you get the risk difference, ten fewer strokes per hundred patients treated.
    text rdT "RD = −10 per 100" #8a5fa8 x 450 y 162

    write id nnt text "**Number needed to treat** $NNT = 1/|RD| = 10$"

Turn it upside down, and it becomes the number needed to treat. Treat ten people for a year, and you prevent one stroke.
    text nntT "NNT = 10" #8a5fa8 x 760 y 162

Laupacis, Sackett and Roberts proposed that number in nineteen eighty-eight, because it speaks in patients rather than proportions.
    highlight nnt part "NNT = 1/|RD| = 10" effect underline

The ninety who were spared on the drug, and the eighty on placebo, will matter soon, because the next measure uses them.
    highlight cell_0_1 cell_1_1

For now, keep one idea. The risk difference and the number needed to treat are absolute measures. They tell you how many people a treatment changes.
    write "> Absolute measures say **how many** people are affected."

    pause 1

## The risk ratio
level: advanced
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 60 % baseline
      - Our trial
      - Low-risk patients
    series:
      - {name: Placebo, values: [60, 20, 2]}
      - {name: Drug, values: [100, 10, 1]}
    value_labels: true
    label_size: 24
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 115
    y_label: Events per 100

    path ceil dashed #8f887c points [[-0.5,100],[2.5,100]] data true hidden true
    label lCeil "risk = 1: RR ≤ 1/0.6 = 1.67" above #8a5fa8 attach_to ceil hidden true

Back in our trial, the one-year risk was twenty percent on placebo and ten on the drug.
    draw axes legend bar_2

Divide the risks instead of subtracting them, and you get the risk ratio, also called the relative risk. Here it is one half.
    write id rr text "**Risk ratio** $RR = \\dfrac{\\text{risk}_1}{\\text{risk}_0} = \\dfrac{0.10}{0.20} = 0.5$"

A risk ratio of one means no difference. Below one, the exposure prevents events, and above one, it causes them.
    write "$RR = 1$: no effect · $RR < 1$: fewer events"

Reported as a relative risk reduction, that becomes the headline. The drug halves the risk of stroke.
    write "**Relative risk reduction** $1 - RR = 50\\%$"

Now keep the drug's effect the same, but give it to patients whose baseline risk is ten times lower.
    highlight bar_2

Two in a hundred of them have a stroke on placebo. If the risk ratio holds, the drug halves that to one in a hundred.
    draw bar_3

    scale nntScale x 300 y 500 width 520 label "Number needed to treat at 2 % baseline" unit " patients" min 0 max 300 value 100

    ask "Same risk ratio, baseline risk 2 %. How many must be treated for a year to prevent one stroke?" on nntScale store nnt
        right: Yes, {nnt.true}. The drug prevents one stroke per hundred treated here, against one per ten in our trial.
        wrong: You said {nnt}; it is {nnt.true}. The risk difference is only one point per hundred, and the number needed to treat is one over it.

    write "| | RR | RD | NNT |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| High risk | 0.5 | 10 % | 10 |\n| Low risk | 0.5 | 1 % | 100 |"

The relative effect is still one half. But the absolute benefit has shrunk from ten percentage points to one.
    erase nntScale

To prevent one stroke in these patients, you must now treat a hundred people instead of ten, with the costs and side effects borne by all of them.
    highlight bar_3

That is the usual division of labour. Risk ratios tend to be fairly stable across populations, so they travel well, while the risk difference depends on the baseline risk.
    write id relabs text "> Relative effects often **travel**; absolute effects **decide**."

But it is the absolute effect that a patient's decision rests on.
    highlight relabs part decide

    write "Bound: $RR \\le 1/p_0$ (with $p_0 = 0.6$, at most $1.67$)"

One more property is easy to forget. A risk cannot exceed one, so when the baseline risk is sixty percent, no risk ratio can be above one point six seven.
    draw bar_1 ceil lCeil

That ceiling is a first hint that ratios behave differently when outcomes are common, and the odds ratio is where it matters most.
    point at.ref ceil gesture underline

    pause 1

chapter: Odds

## Odds
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,1],"y":[0,5]}
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

    axes ax x_label "Risk p" y_label Odds hidden true
    curve odds #b5482e expr x/(1-x) x_from 0 x_to 0.833 hidden true
    label lOdds "odds" left #b5482e attach_to odds hidden true
    dot pA at.on odds at.x 0.2 guides.values true hidden true
    dot pB at.on odds at.x 0.5 guides.values true hidden true
    dot pC at.on odds at.x 0.8 guides.x "0.8" guides.y "4" hidden true
    dot p10 at.on odds at.x 0.1 guides.x "0.1" hidden true
    label lP10 "odds 0.11" above-left attach_to p10 hidden true
    dot r80 #2f6b8f at.on diag at.x 0.8 hidden true
    label lR80 "risk 0.8" below-right #2f6b8f attach_to r80 hidden true

Risk is not the only way to put a number on a chance. Gamblers use odds, and so does logistic regression.
    draw ax

    write id oddsdef text "**Odds** $= \\dfrac{p}{1-p}$"

The odds compare the chance that an event happens with the chance that it does not.
    draw odds lOdds

A risk of twenty percent is twenty against eighty, odds of one to four, or zero point two five.
    draw pA

A risk of one half gives even odds, exactly one.
    draw pB

And a risk of eighty percent is eighty against twenty, odds of four.
    draw pC

To go back, divide the odds by one plus the odds. Four over five is eighty percent again.
    write "Back: $p = \\dfrac{\\text{odds}}{1+\\text{odds}} = \\dfrac{4}{5} = 0.8$"

    ask "What are the odds when the risk is 90 %?" estimate.min 0 estimate.max 30 estimate.value 9 stage own store o90
        right: Nine: ninety against ten. The odds run away as the risk nears one.
        wrong: You said {o90}; it is {o90.true}. Divide zero point nine by zero point one.

So the odds run from zero to infinity, and they climb ever more steeply as the risk approaches one.
    erase estimate_1

Now set the risk itself beside them, as a straight line.
    curve diag dashed #2f6b8f expr x x_from 0 x_to 1
    label lDiag "risk" below-right #2f6b8f attach_to diag

Down where the risk is small, the two almost coincide.
    camera center.x 0.25 center.y 0.8 zoom 1.6

A risk of ten percent has odds of zero point one one.
    draw p10 lP10

That is the rare-outcome rule. When the event is rare, odds and risk are nearly the same number.
    write id rare text "**Rare outcome**: odds $\\approx$ risk"

Higher up they part fast.
    camera reset true

At a risk of eighty percent, the odds are five times larger.
    draw r80 lR80

    point at.ref pC gesture circle

    write "Our trial: drug $\\dfrac{10}{90} = 0.11$, placebo $\\dfrac{20}{80} = 0.25$"

In our trial, the odds of stroke were ten to ninety on the drug, and twenty to eighty on placebo.
    highlight p10

Placebo sits right here, at a risk of twenty percent and odds of a quarter. The next step is to divide one odds by the other.
    highlight pA

    pause 1

## The odds ratio
level: advanced
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: Group
    col_label: Outcome at 1 year
    row_values:
      - 'Drug: 100'
      - 'Placebo: 100'
    col_values:
      - Stroke
      - No stroke
    cells:
      - ['10', '90']
      - ['20', '80']
    cell_notes:
      - [a, b]
      - [c, d]

    text oddsD "odds 0.11" #2f6b8f x 900 y 461 hidden true
    text oddsP "odds 0.25" #b5482e x 900 y 280 hidden true

Divide one group's odds by the other's, and you have the odds ratio, the measure behind case-control studies and logistic regression.
    draw grid row_title col_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1 cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 oddsD oddsP

    write id or text "$OR = \\dfrac{a/b}{c/d} = \\dfrac{ad}{bc}$"

In the cells of a two-by-two table it is a times d over b times c, the cross-product ratio.
    highlight cell_0_0 cell_1_1

    highlight cell_0_1 cell_1_0

    pause 1

    write "Our trial: $OR = \\dfrac{10 \\times 80}{90 \\times 20} = 0.44$, while $RR = 0.50$"

For our stroke trial it comes to zero point four four, a little further from one than the risk ratio of one half.
    text orT "OR = 0.11 / 0.25 = 0.44" #8a5fa8 x 580 y 140

    write id logit text "$$\\log\\frac{p}{1-p} = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$$"

The same quantity sits at the heart of logistic regression, which models the log of the odds as a straight line in the covariates.
    point at.ref oddsP gesture underline

    write "So $e^{\\beta_1} = OR$; for the drug, $\\beta_1 = \\ln 0.44 \\approx -0.81$"

Each coefficient is a log odds ratio. For the drug in our trial it is about minus zero point eight one. Exponentiate it, and you are back at zero point four four.
    point at.ref orT gesture underline

So whenever a paper reports a logistic model, its effects are odds ratios, whatever the abstract calls them.
    highlight logit part "\\beta_1 x" effect underline

    pause 1

## Case-control studies
level: advanced
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: Smoked?
    col_label: Men sampled as
    row_values:
      - Smoker
      - Non-smoker
    col_values:
      - 'Lung cancer: 649'
      - 'Controls: 649'
    cells:
      - ['647', '622']
      - ['2', '27']
    cell_notes:
      - [a, b]
      - [c, d]
sources:
    - id: dollhill
      title: Smoking and carcinoma of the lung; preliminary report
      authors: Doll R, Hill AB
      year: 1950
      finding: Of 649 men with lung carcinoma, 2 were non-smokers; of 649 matched control patients, 27 were (BMJ 1950;2:739–48). Odds ratio ≈ 14.
    - id: cornfield
      title: A method of estimating comparative rates from clinical data; applications to cancer of the lung, breast, and cervix
      authors: Cornfield J
      year: 1951
      finding: In a case-control study the exposure odds ratio estimates the relative risk when the disease is rare.

Its real strength shows when a disease is rare and would take decades to follow. A case-control study starts from the disease, not from the exposure.
    draw grid row_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_title col_header_0 col_header_1

The classic case is Doll and Hill's study of lung cancer, published in nineteen fifty. Of six hundred and forty-nine men with lung cancer in London hospitals, all but two had smoked.
    draw cell_0_0 cell_1_0

Each was matched with a control patient without cancer. Among those six hundred and forty-nine men, twenty-seven had never smoked.
    draw cell_0_1 cell_1_1

    write id fakerisk text "Risk among smokers? $\\dfrac{647}{647 + 622} = 51\\%$" temp true

Now try to compute a risk. Six hundred and forty-seven cancers among twelve hundred and sixty-nine smokers looks like a risk of fifty-one percent.
    highlight cell_0_0 cell_0_1

But that number is an artefact. Take two controls per case and it drops to about a third, because the investigators chose how many controls there were.
    highlight fakerisk effect strike

    scale orScale x 250 y 95 width 500 label "Odds ratio, smokers vs non-smokers" min 0 max 40 value 14

    ask "From this table, roughly what is the odds ratio for lung cancer in smokers?" on orScale store ordh
        right: About fourteen: six hundred and forty-seven times twenty-seven, over six hundred and twenty-two times two.
        wrong: You said {ordh}; it is about {ordh.true}. Cross-multiply: a times d over b times c.

    write id orcc text "Doll & Hill: $OR = \\dfrac{647 \\times 27}{622 \\times 2} \\approx 14$"

The odds ratio does not care.
    erase orScale

Six hundred and forty-seven times twenty-seven, over six hundred and twenty-two times two, is about fourteen.
    highlight cell_0_0 cell_1_1

Doubling the controls doubles b and d alike, and the factor cancels in a times d over b times c.
    focus cell_0_1 cell_1_1

    write "> The exposure OR equals the disease OR."

The odds ratio is symmetric. The odds of smoking in cases against controls equal the odds of cancer in smokers against non-smokers.
    point at.ref row_title gesture underline

    write "*Cornfield, 1951: for a rare disease, OR ≈ RR.*"

Jerome Cornfield showed this in nineteen fifty-one, with lung cancer among his examples, and added that when the disease is rare, the odds ratio also approximates the risk ratio.
    point at.ref col_header_0 gesture underline

    write "*Only two non-smoking cases: a rough estimate, but nowhere near 1.*"

With only two non-smoking cases, fourteen is a rough estimate. But no plausible error brings it anywhere near one.
    point at.ref cell_1_0 gesture circle

    pause 1

## When the odds ratio misleads
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,1.25],"y":[0,2.5]}
vars: {"k":2}
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}
sources:
    - id: zhangyu
      title: What's the relative risk? A method of correcting the odds ratio in cohort studies of common outcomes
      authors: Zhang J, Yu KF
      year: 1998
      finding: RR = OR / (1 − P0 + P0 × OR); the more common the outcome, the more the OR overstates the RR.
      doi: 10.1001/jama.280.19.1690
    - id: schwartz
      title: Misunderstandings about the effects of race and sex on physicians' referrals for cardiac catheterization
      authors: Schwartz LM, Woloshin S, Welch HG
      year: 1999
      finding: An odds ratio of 0.6 was reported as '40 % less often'; referral rates were 85 % vs 91 %, a risk ratio of about 0.93.
      doi: 10.1056/NEJM199907223410411
    - id: mcnutt
      title: Estimating the relative risk in cohort studies and clinical trials of common outcomes
      authors: McNutt LA, Wu C, Xue X, Hafner JP
      year: 2003
      finding: Applying the Zhang–Yu formula to adjusted odds ratios is biased; log-binomial or Poisson regression estimate the RR directly.
      doi: 10.1093/aje/kwg074

    axes ax x_label "Baseline risk p0" y_label Ratio hidden true
    curve one dashed #8f887c expr "1" x_from 0 x_to 1.2 hidden true
    curve orline #b5482e expr k x_from 0 x_to 1 hidden true
    label lOr "OR = {k:1}" above-right #b5482e attach_to orline hidden true
    label lOne "no effect" below-right #8f887c attach_to one hidden true
    curve rr #2f6b8f expr "k/(1 - x + x*k)" x_from 0 x_to 1 cites ["zhangyu"] hidden true
    label lRr "RR" above-right #2f6b8f attach_to rr hidden true
    dot p05 at.on rr at.x 0.05 guides.values true hidden true
    dot p50 at.on rr at.x 0.5 guides.values true hidden true
    dot pS #8a5fa8 at.x 0.91 at.y 0.934 guides.y "0.93" cites ["schwartz"] hidden true
    label lPS "85 % vs 91 %" below-left #8a5fa8 attach_to pS hidden true

So when does the odds ratio stop approximating the risk ratio? The answer depends on how common the outcome is.
    draw ax

Fix the odds ratio at two, the same in every population, and let the baseline risk run from zero to one.
    draw orline lOr one lOne

For a baseline risk p-zero, the matching risk ratio follows from this formula, which Zhang and Yu popularised in nineteen ninety-eight.
    write id zy text "$$RR = \\frac{OR}{1 - p_0 + p_0 \\cdot OR}$$"

    scale rrScale x 250 y 440 width 500 label "RR when p0 = 50 %" min 1 max 2.2 value 1.33

    ask "The odds ratio is 2. If half the unexposed have the outcome, where is the risk ratio? Click the line." on rrScale stage own store rr50
        right: Yes, about {rr50.true}: two over one point five. A common outcome pulls the risk ratio well below the odds ratio.
        wrong: You said {rr50}; it is {rr50.true}. Put p-zero one half into the formula: two over one minus a half plus one.

    erase rrScale

Drawn out, the risk ratio starts at two when the outcome is rare, and sinks toward one as it becomes common.
    draw rr lRr

At a baseline risk of five percent, the risk ratio is still one point nine, and the odds ratio is a fair stand-in.
    draw p05

At fifty percent it is only one point three three. Twice the odds is a third more risk.
    draw p50

The odds ratio always lies further from one than the risk ratio, above one and below it, and the gap grows with the baseline risk.
    write id further text "> The OR is always **further from 1** than the RR."

Now turn the same picture over, to an odds ratio below one.
    erase p05 p50

Set the odds ratio to zero point six. Where the outcome is common, the risk ratio climbs almost to one.
    animate k 0.6 duration 3 ghost ["rr"]

That is the scale of a real mix-up. In nineteen ninety-nine, a study reported that doctors referred Black patients for cardiac catheterization with an odds ratio of zero point six, compared with white patients.
    erase rr_ghost

    write "Referred: 85 % vs 91 % → $RR = 0.93$, but $OR \\approx 0.6$"

News reports said Black patients were forty percent less likely to be referred. But the referral rates were eighty-five and ninety-one percent.
    draw pS lPS

That is a risk ratio of about zero point nine three, a seven percent difference, as Schwartz, Woloshin and Welch pointed out in the same journal.
    highlight pS

One warning about the conversion. Fed an adjusted odds ratio, it gives biased estimates and wrong confidence intervals, so with a common outcome, estimate the risk ratio directly, with a log-binomial or a modified Poisson model.
    write "*Caveat*: with an **adjusted** OR the conversion is biased. Use log-binomial or Poisson models."

So with a common outcome, read an odds ratio as an exaggerated risk ratio, in whichever direction it points.
    highlight further part "further from 1"

    pause 1

chapter: Time

## Rates and person-time
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,6.6],"y":[0,7]}
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

    axes ax x_label "Years of follow-up" y_label Patient cross corner hidden true
    path f1 #3d3833 points [[0,6],[5,6]] data true hidden true
    path f2 #3d3833 points [[0,5],[2,5]] data true hidden true
    path f3 #3d3833 points [[0,4],[5,4]] data true hidden true
    path f4 #3d3833 points [[0,3],[3,3]] data true hidden true
    path f5 #3d3833 points [[0,2],[1,2]] data true hidden true
    path f6 #3d3833 points [[0,1],[4,1]] data true hidden true
    dot e2 #b5482e at.x 2 at.y 5 hidden true
    dot e4 #b5482e at.x 3 at.y 3 hidden true
    dot e5 #b5482e at.x 1 at.y 2 hidden true
    dot c1 #8f887c at.x 5 at.y 6 hidden true
    dot c3 #8f887c at.x 5 at.y 4 hidden true
    dot c6 #8f887c at.x 4 at.y 1 hidden true
    path t1 #3d3833 points [[1,0],[1,0.18]] data true hidden true
    label lt1 "1" below attach_to t1 hidden true
    path t2 #3d3833 points [[2,0],[2,0.18]] data true hidden true
    label lt2 "2" below attach_to t2 hidden true
    path t3 #3d3833 points [[3,0],[3,0.18]] data true hidden true
    label lt3 "3" below attach_to t3 hidden true
    path t4 #3d3833 points [[4,0],[4,0.18]] data true hidden true
    label lt4 "4" below attach_to t4 hidden true
    path t5 #3d3833 points [[5,0],[5,0.18]] data true hidden true
    label lt5 "5" below attach_to t5 hidden true
    label y1 "5" right attach_to f1 hidden true
    label y2 "2" right attach_to f2 hidden true
    label y3 "5" right attach_to f3 hidden true
    label y4 "3" right attach_to f4 hidden true
    label y5 "1" right attach_to f5 hidden true
    label y6 "4" right attach_to f6 hidden true
    text sum "= 20" #8a5fa8 x 905 y 380 hidden true
    polygon slice #8a5fa8 points [[2,0.3],[3,0.3],[3,6.6],[2,6.6]] data true style.fill #8a5fa8 style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    polygon slice2 #8a5fa8 points [[2.45,0.3],[2.55,0.3],[2.55,6.6],[2.45,6.6]] data true style.fill #8a5fa8 style.fill_style wash style.opacity 0.6 hidden true

Risks and odds both count people. But in a real cohort, people are followed for different lengths of time.
    draw ax t1 t2 t3 t4 t5 lt1 lt2 lt3 lt4 lt5 f1 f2 f3 f4 f5 f6

Here are six patients followed after a first heart attack, an illustration. Three have a second one, after one, two and three years.
    draw e5 e2 e4 parallel true

The others leave without it. The study ends, or they move away, and their follow-up is censored.
    draw c1 c3 c6 parallel true

Three events among six people looks like a risk of fifty percent. But over what time? One patient was watched for a year, others for five.
    write id naive text "Risk? $3/6 = 50\\%$, over *what* time?" temp true

The fix is to count time instead of people. Add up every year each patient was watched.
    flow f1 f2 f3 f4 f5 f6 spacing 114 speed 114

    write "**Person-time** $5+2+5+3+1+4 = 20$ person-years"

These six contribute twenty person-years between them.
    draw y1 y2 y3 y4 y5 y6 sum parallel true

Three events in twenty person-years is a rate of zero point one five per person-year, or fifteen per hundred.
    write id rate text "**Incidence rate** $\\lambda = \\dfrac{3}{20} = 0.15$ per person-year"

Two groups are compared by their rate ratio, which, like the risk ratio, is one when the exposure does nothing.
    write "**Rate ratio** $= \\lambda_1 / \\lambda_0$"

Ten events in five hundred person-years against ten in two hundred and fifty is a rate ratio of one half, even though the counts are equal.
    write "Equal counts, unequal time: $\\dfrac{10}{500}$ vs $\\dfrac{10}{250}$ person-years → $0.5$"

With a constant rate, a rate turns back into a risk. After five years at zero point one five, the risk is one minus e to the minus zero point seven five, about fifty-three percent.
    write id conv text "Constant rate: $\\text{risk}(t) = 1 - e^{-\\lambda t}$"

Person-time has one assumption built in. The censored patients must be like those who stayed, because if the sickest drop out, the rate looks better than it is.
    highlight c6

Now imagine counting events per person-time in an ever thinner slice of time.
    draw slice

In the limit, the rate at a single instant is called the hazard.
    morph slice to.ref slice2 duration 4

    pause 1

## The hazard ratio
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,32],"y":[0,1.05]}
vars:
    h:
      value: 0.5
      min: 0.2
      max: 1.5
      step: 0.05
      decimals: 2
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}
sources:
    - id: cox
      title: Regression models and life-tables
      authors: Cox DR
      year: 1972
      finding: The proportional hazards model h(t|x) = h0(t)·exp(βx), estimated by partial likelihood without specifying h0(t).
    - id: hernan
      title: The hazards of hazard ratios
      authors: Hernán MA
      year: 2010
      finding: A period-specific hazard ratio compares survivors, who need not be comparable even in a randomized trial; report survival curves.

    axes ax x_label Years y_label "Share dead" hidden true
    curve s0 #b5482e expr "1 - exp(-0.2*x)" x_from 0 x_to 30 hidden true
    label lS0 "Standard" above-left #b5482e attach_to s0 hidden true
    curve s1 #2f6b8f expr "1 - exp(-0.2*h*x)" x_from 0 x_to 30 cites ["cox"] hidden true
    label lS1 "New drug" below-right #2f6b8f attach_to s1 hidden true
    math hrK at.data [24,0.55] tex "HR = {h}" size 34 hidden true
    path yt5 #3d3833 points [[0,0.5],[0.6,0.5]] data true hidden true
    label lyt5 "0.5" left attach_to yt5 hidden true
    path yt1 #3d3833 points [[0,1],[0.6,1]] data true hidden true
    label lyt1 "1" left attach_to yt1 hidden true
    scale rrScale10 x 500 y 200 width 400 label "Risk ratio of death at 10 years" min 0 max 1 value 0.73 hidden true

Shrink that slice of time to an instant, and the rate becomes the hazard, the event rate among those still event-free, right now.
    draw ax yt5 lyt5 yt1 lyt1

    write id hz text "**Hazard** $h(t) = \\lim\\limits_{\\Delta t \\to 0} \\dfrac{P(t \\le T < t + \\Delta t \\mid T \\ge t)}{\\Delta t}$"

Take patients with an advanced cancer, and say that on standard treatment they die at a constant hazard of zero point two a year, an illustration.
    draw s0

Drawn as the share who have died, their curve rises as one minus e to the minus zero point two t.
    draw lS0

The hazard ratio compares two groups' hazards at the same moment.
    write id hr text "**Hazard ratio** $HR = h_1(t) / h_0(t)$"

    animate h 1 duration 0.1

Now add a new drug, and start it at a hazard ratio of one, where its curve lies on top of the standard one.
    draw s1 lS1 hrK

Then lower the hazard ratio to one half. At every moment, patients on the new drug die at half the standard rate.
    animate h 0.5 duration 3

This is the Cox proportional hazards model, from nineteen seventy-two. The baseline hazard is left unspecified, and because it cancels in the ratio, the hazard ratio needs no model of time at all.
    write id coxm text "**Cox model** $h(t \\mid x) = h_0(t)\\, e^{\\beta x}$, so $HR = e^{\\beta}$"

Now the trap. After two years, thirty-three percent of the standard group have died, against eighteen percent on the new drug.
    dot a2 at.on s0 at.x 2 guides.values true
    dot b2 at.on s1 at.x 2 guides.y "0.18"
    parallel true

    write "Year 2: deaths 33 % vs 18 % → $RR = 0.55$"

    draw rrScale10

    ask "The hazard ratio stays 0.5. Read the curves: what is the risk ratio of death at ten years?" on rrScale10 store rr10
        right: About {rr10.true}: sixty-three over eighty-six. The ratio of deaths drifts toward one.
        wrong: You said {rr10}; it is {rr10.true}. Read both curves at ten years and divide.

    erase rrScale10

After ten years it is eighty-six against sixty-three percent, a risk ratio of zero point seven three.
    dot a10 at.on s0 at.x 10 guides.values true
    dot b10 at.on s1 at.x 10 guides.y "0.63"
    parallel true

The hazard ratio stayed at one half the whole time, yet the risk ratio drifted toward one.
    write "Year 10: deaths 86 % vs 63 % → $RR = 0.73$"

    write id hrnot text "> HR = 0.5: half the rate **at each moment**, not half as many deaths."

Everyone dies in the end. A drug that halves the hazard delays deaths, so the share who have died converges.
    point at.data [28,0.97] gesture circle

Under proportional hazards, survival on the new drug is simply the standard group's survival raised to the power of the hazard ratio.
    write "Under proportional hazards: $S_1(t) = S_0(t)^{HR}$"

Hernán added a subtler warning in twenty ten. Later hazards compare the survivors, and after the first deaths, the survivors in two arms are no longer alike, even in a randomized trial.
    highlight hrnot part "at each moment"

    pause 1

## When hazards are not proportional
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,10],"y":[0,1]}
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

    axes ax x_label Years y_label Survival hidden true
    curve med #b5482e expr exp(-0.08*x) x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    label lMed "Medical" below-left #b5482e attach_to med hidden true
    curve surg #2f6b8f expr "exp(-(0.2*(1-exp(-5*x)) + 0.03*x))" x_from 0 x_to 10 hidden true
    label lSurg "Surgery" above-right #2f6b8f attach_to surg hidden true
    dot cross at.on med at.x 4 guides.values true hidden true
    region loss x_from 0 x_to 4 between ["med","surg"] style.fill #8f887c hidden true
    region gain x_from 4 x_to 10 between ["med","surg"] style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    text lossT "−0.3 yr" #3d3833 x 215 y 588 hidden true
    text gainT "+0.5 yr" #2f6b8f x 768 y 431 hidden true
    text hrLate "HR ≈ 0.4" #8a5fa8 x 800 y 540 hidden true

The Cox model assumes the hazard ratio is the same at every moment. Often it is not.
    draw ax

Take a made-up comparison. A medical treatment leaves patients with a steady death rate of eight percent a year.
    draw med lMed

Surgery kills some patients early, around the operation, but leaves the survivors with a hazard of only three percent a year.
    draw surg lSurg

In the first months the surgical hazard is far higher, so the hazard ratio is well above one.
    text hrEarly "HR > 1" #8a5fa8 x 250 y 470

Later it settles near zero point four, and the survival curves cross, here at four years.
    draw cross hrLate

That breaks the proportional hazards assumption.
    write id ph text "**Proportional hazards**: $h_1(t)/h_0(t)$ constant in $t$. Here it is not."

A single Cox hazard ratio would average an early harm and a late benefit, weighted by when the deaths happen, and describe neither.
    write "One Cox $HR$ = a weighted average of early harm and late benefit."

To check the assumption, plot log minus log survival against log time and look for parallel lines, or test the Schoenfeld residuals for a trend.
    write "Check: $\\log(-\\log S)$ against $\\log t$ parallel? Schoenfeld residuals flat?"

    write id rmst text "**RMST**: area under $S(t)$ up to a horizon $\\tau$"

When it fails, report the curves themselves, hazard ratios for separate periods, or the restricted mean survival time, the area under each curve up to a fixed horizon.
    erase hrEarly hrLate

Up to four years, surgery loses area, about three tenths of a year of life on average.
    draw loss lossT

After four years it gains it back, just over half a year.
    draw gain gainT

Over ten years, the net difference is about a fifth of a year, roughly ten weeks, in favour of surgery.
    write "At $\\tau = 10$: $7.11 - 6.88 = 0.22$ years in favour of surgery"

That number is in time, not in ratios, and it stays meaningful whether hazards are proportional or not.
    point at.ref gain gesture circle

    pause 1

chapter: "Choosing a measure"

## Collapsibility, and which measure to use
level: advanced
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Older
      - Younger
      - Everyone
    series:
      - {name: Placebo, values: [50, 20, 35]}
      - {name: Treated, values: [80, 50, 65]}
    value_labels: true
    label_size: 24
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 110
    y_label: Recovered, %
sources:
    - id: greenland
      title: Confounding and collapsibility in causal inference
      authors: Greenland S, Robins JM, Pearl J
      year: 1999
      finding: The odds ratio is not collapsible; a marginal OR can differ from the common stratum-specific OR without any confounding.

    text or1 "OR 4" #8a5fa8 x 255 y 700 hidden true
    text or2 "OR 4" #8a5fa8 x 525 y 700 hidden true
    text or3 "OR 3.45" #8a5fa8 x 795 y 700 hidden true

One more property separates these measures, and it surprises experienced analysts. Take a trial with two equal strata, randomized within each, so there is no confounding.
    draw axes legend

Say the outcome is recovery from pneumonia within a week, with made-up numbers. Among older patients, recovery is fifty percent on placebo and eighty on treatment, an odds ratio of four.
    draw bar_1 or1

Among younger patients it is twenty against fifty percent, again an odds ratio of exactly four.
    draw bar_2 or2

Pool everyone, and recovery is thirty-five against sixty-five percent. The odds ratio is now three point four five.
    draw bar_3 or3

So the pooled odds ratio is smaller than the odds ratio in every stratum, with no confounding at all.
    write "| | RR | OR |\n|---|---|---|\n| Older | 1.6 | 4 |\n| Younger | 2.5 | 4 |\n| Everyone | 1.86 | 3.45 |"

That is non-collapsibility. The risk ratio and the risk difference pool to values between the strata, but the odds ratio and the hazard ratio do not.
    write id noncoll text "**Non-collapsible**: the marginal OR is not an average of the stratum ORs."

So adding a prognostic covariate to a logistic or a Cox model moves the estimate away from one, even in a perfectly randomized trial.
    highlight bar_3

That change is not confounding. The conditional and the marginal odds ratios simply answer different questions.
    highlight noncoll part Non-collapsible

Put together, each measure answers its own question, and only some designs can deliver it.
    erase bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 or1 or2 or3 legend axes

    cards ccSort
        Case-control can: Odds ratio, Odds of exposure
        Needs follow-up: Risk, Risk difference, NNT

    ask "Which measures can a case-control study give you on its own? Sort each card." on ccSort stage own store cc
        right: {cc} right on the first try.
        wrong: {cc} of {cc.total} on the first try. A case-control study fixes how many cases and controls there are, so only odds survive.

    erase ccSort

    write "| Measure | Answers | Design |\n|---|---|---|\n| RD, NNT | how many | cohort, trial |\n| RR | how many times | cohort, trial |\n| OR | odds; symmetric | also case-control |\n| Rate ratio | events per time | cohort |\n| HR | rate at each moment | survival, Cox |"

A case-control study gives you the odds ratio alone, unless the baseline risk is known from elsewhere, while a trial or a cohort can give you every one of them.
    write "*Case-control: the OR only, unless the baseline risk is known.*"

One habit protects readers from most of these traps. Give the absolute risks, at a stated time, next to every ratio.
    write id habit text "> Report the **absolute risks**, at a stated time, beside every ratio."

## A last check
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,12],"y":[0,1.05]}
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

    axes ax x_label Years y_label "Share dead" hidden true
    curve c0 #b5482e expr "1 - exp(-0.1609*x)" x_from 0 x_to 11 hidden true
    label lC0 "Control" above-left #b5482e attach_to c0 hidden true
    curve c1 #2f6b8f expr "1 - exp(-0.0805*x)" x_from 0 x_to 11 hidden true
    label lC1 "New drug, HR 0.5" below-right #2f6b8f attach_to c1 hidden true
    dot q0 at.on c0 at.x 10 guides.values true hidden true
    dot q1 at.on c1 at.x 10 guides.y "0.55" hidden true

One last check, on a new cancer trial, drawn the way we drew the hazards.
    draw ax c0 lC0

In the control arm, eighty percent have died by ten years.
    draw q0

    quiz "With a hazard ratio of 0.5 and proportional hazards, how many on the new drug have died by 10 years?"
        right: About fifty-five percent. Control survival is zero point two, and zero point two to the power of one half is zero point four five, so fifty-five percent have died: far from half of eighty.
        wrong: A hazard ratio acts on the rate at each moment, not on the share who have died. Use survival, raised to the power of the hazard ratio.
        * About 40 %
        + About 55 %
        * About 80 %

On the curves, eighty percent dead in the control arm becomes about fifty-five on the new drug.
    draw c1 lC1 q1

Not forty. Halving the hazard slows the deaths; it does not halve them.
    path naiveL dashed #8f887c points [[9.3,0.4],[10.7,0.4]] data true
    label lNaive "not 0.4" right #8f887c attach_to naiveL

So read every ratio as an answer to its own question, and ask for the absolute risks that say how many people it changes.
    write "> Ratios say how much faster or likelier; absolute risks say **how many**."

    pause 1
---
audio:
  lang: en
  lines:
    '|a||Odds, risks and hazards':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3480
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||How risk, odds and hazard ratios differ, when they agree, and why twice the odds is rarely twice the risk, worked through a stroke trial, a lung-cancer case-control study and survival curves.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14800
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:21ca9k'
    '|a||A paper can report a treatment''s effect as a risk ratio, an odds ratio or a hazard ratio. They sound alike, but they answer different questions, and the gap between them can be large.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 16280
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||All of them start from the same place, a two-by-two table of exposure against outcome.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6560
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1tb4npn'
    '|a||Take a made-up trial. A hundred patients get the drug, and ten of them have a stroke within a year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1ai7q0b'
    '|a||Another hundred get placebo, and twenty of them have a stroke.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4600
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1pmmpt0'
    '|a||The plainest measure is the risk, the share of the people at the start who have the event.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5840
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:14emhyt'
    '|a||So the risk is ten percent on the drug, and twenty percent on placebo.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5880
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:431o31'
    '|a||A risk without a time window means nothing, so we say one-year risk.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5320
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:165loh6'
    '|a||Subtract one risk from the other, and you get the risk difference, ten fewer strokes per hundred patients treated.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8160
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:p05mv1'
    '|a||Turn it upside down, and it becomes the number needed to treat. Treat ten people for a year, and you prevent one stroke.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:tjdncw'
    '|a||Laupacis, Sackett and Roberts proposed that number in nineteen eighty-eight, because it speaks in patients rather than proportions.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9320
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:178szj7'
    '|a||The ninety who were spared on the drug, and the eighty on placebo, will matter soon, because the next measure uses them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8400
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:dkdbfr'
    '|a||For now, keep one idea. The risk difference and the number needed to treat are absolute measures. They tell you how many people a treatment changes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10400
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:l854vt'
    '|a||Back in our trial, the one-year risk was twenty percent on placebo and ten on the drug.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7320
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Divide the risks instead of subtracting them, and you get the risk ratio, also called the relative risk. Here it is one half.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9360
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:79devk'
    '|a||A risk ratio of one means no difference. Below one, the exposure prevents events, and above one, it causes them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1uv0yb2'
    '|a||Reported as a relative risk reduction, that becomes the headline. The drug halves the risk of stroke.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7200
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:139l58k'
    '|a||Now keep the drug''s effect the same, but give it to patients whose baseline risk is ten times lower.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7160
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1otgg55'
    '|a||Two in a hundred of them have a stroke on placebo. If the risk ratio holds, the drug halves that to one in a hundred.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7960
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:9w4h4'
    '|a||Same risk ratio, baseline risk 2 %. How many must be treated for a year to prevent one stroke?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8120
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1qgat2f'
    '|a||The relative effect is still one half. But the absolute benefit has shrunk from ten percentage points to one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9280
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||To prevent one stroke in these patients, you must now treat a hundred people instead of ten, with the costs and side effects borne by all of them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10320
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:bwcfqh'
    '|a||That is the usual division of labour. Risk ratios tend to be fairly stable across populations, so they travel well, while the risk difference depends on the baseline risk.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:ggz9sh'
    '|a||But it is the absolute effect that a patient''s decision rests on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5080
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1iws2wr'
    '|a||One more property is easy to forget. A risk cannot exceed one, so when the baseline risk is sixty percent, no risk ratio can be above one point six seven.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:ca0tw8'
    '|a||That ceiling is a first hint that ratios behave differently when outcomes are common, and the odds ratio is where it matters most.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8960
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:181mt52'
    '|a||Next chapter: Odds':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2640
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Risk is not the only way to put a number on a chance. Gamblers use odds, and so does logistic regression.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8760
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||The odds compare the chance that an event happens with the chance that it does not.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5560
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:p5djcl'
    '|a||A risk of twenty percent is twenty against eighty, odds of one to four, or zero point two five.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9800
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:4fyq1b'
    '|a||A risk of one half gives even odds, exactly one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5320
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:16jmgge'
    '|a||And a risk of eighty percent is eighty against twenty, odds of four.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5560
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:bujal4'
    '|a||To go back, divide the odds by one plus the odds. Four over five is eighty percent again.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7920
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1xmtrps'
    '|a||What are the odds when the risk is 90 %?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3280
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:86ueb'
    '|a||So the odds run from zero to infinity, and they climb ever more steeply as the risk approaches one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8440
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Now set the risk itself beside them, as a straight line.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4240
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:xdokee'
    '|a||Down where the risk is small, the two almost coincide.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4320
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:nlfiy2'
    '|a||A risk of ten percent has odds of zero point one one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4720
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:avzeg5'
    '|a||That is the rare-outcome rule. When the event is rare, odds and risk are nearly the same number.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6720
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:13m42re'
    '|a||Higher up they part fast.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2320
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:f2okbr'
    '|a||At a risk of eighty percent, the odds are five times larger.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4600
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1cn6801'
    '|a||In our trial, the odds of stroke were ten to ninety on the drug, and twenty to eighty on placebo.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:5xbmqz'
    '|a||Placebo sits right here, at a risk of twenty percent and odds of a quarter. The next step is to divide one odds by the other.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9280
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:2jrpj1'
    '|a||Divide one group''s odds by the other''s, and you have the odds ratio, the measure behind case-control studies and logistic regression.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9800
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||In the cells of a two-by-two table it is a times d over b times c, the cross-product ratio.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8080
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1e7u2n8'
    '|a||For our stroke trial it comes to zero point four four, a little further from one than the risk ratio of one half.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8320
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:kip2i7'
    '|a||The same quantity sits at the heart of logistic regression, which models the log of the odds as a straight line in the covariates.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8440
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:108o42f'
    '|a||Each coefficient is a log odds ratio. For the drug in our trial it is about minus zero point eight one. Exponentiate it, and you are back at zero point four four.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12560
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1rdm3op'
    '|a||So whenever a paper reports a logistic model, its effects are odds ratios, whatever the abstract calls them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7680
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1ehzupr'
    '|a||Its real strength shows when a disease is rare and would take decades to follow. A case-control study starts from the disease, not from the exposure.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10080
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||The classic case is Doll and Hill''s study of lung cancer, published in nineteen fifty. Of six hundred and forty-nine men with lung cancer in London hospitals, all but two had smoked.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13600
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1t1d26h'
    '|a||Each was matched with a control patient without cancer. Among those six hundred and forty-nine men, twenty-seven had never smoked.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9280
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1dwbs7o'
    '|a||Now try to compute a risk. Six hundred and forty-seven cancers among twelve hundred and sixty-nine smokers looks like a risk of fifty-one percent.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:5bpcv3'
    '|a||But that number is an artefact. Take two controls per case and it drops to about a third, because the investigators chose how many controls there were.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10680
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1u63bn1'
    '|a||From this table, roughly what is the odds ratio for lung cancer in smokers?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5600
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:bt98hv'
    '|a||The odds ratio does not care.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2800
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Six hundred and forty-seven times twenty-seven, over six hundred and twenty-two times two, is about fourteen.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8560
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1glh8kn'
    '|a||Doubling the controls doubles b and d alike, and the factor cancels in a times d over b times c.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9040
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1c0e0y2'
    '|a||The odds ratio is symmetric. The odds of smoking in cases against controls equal the odds of cancer in smokers against non-smokers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:unlomh'
    '|a||Jerome Cornfield showed this in nineteen fifty-one, with lung cancer among his examples, and added that when the disease is rare, the odds ratio also approximates the risk ratio.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11880
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:tkjdwu'
    '|a||With only two non-smoking cases, fourteen is a rough estimate. But no plausible error brings it anywhere near one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8480
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:19oc4bd'
    '|a||So when does the odds ratio stop approximating the risk ratio? The answer depends on how common the outcome is.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8680
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Fix the odds ratio at two, the same in every population, and let the baseline risk run from zero to one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7960
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:829axf'
    '|a||For a baseline risk p-zero, the matching risk ratio follows from this formula, which Zhang and Yu popularised in nineteen ninety-eight.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10320
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:pvoo5h'
    '|a||The odds ratio is 2. If half the unexposed have the outcome, where is the risk ratio? Click the line.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8440
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:und2pt'
    '|a||Drawn out, the risk ratio starts at two when the outcome is rare, and sinks toward one as it becomes common.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8600
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||At a baseline risk of five percent, the risk ratio is still one point nine, and the odds ratio is a fair stand-in.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8720
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:61corc'
    '|a||At fifty percent it is only one point three three. Twice the odds is a third more risk.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7040
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:c4qaf7'
    '|a||The odds ratio always lies further from one than the risk ratio, above one and below it, and the gap grows with the baseline risk.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10600
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1mc66ok'
    '|a||Now turn the same picture over, to an odds ratio below one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5200
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:fumkzd'
    '|a||Set the odds ratio to zero point six. Where the outcome is common, the risk ratio climbs almost to one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8160
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:c45ktm'
    '|a||That is the scale of a real mix-up. In nineteen ninety-nine, a study reported that doctors referred Black patients for cardiac catheterization with an odds ratio of zero point six, compared with white patients.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14960
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:whuyiw'
    '|a||News reports said Black patients were forty percent less likely to be referred. But the referral rates were eighty-five and ninety-one percent.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10560
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1v9zdo4'
    '|a||That is a risk ratio of about zero point nine three, a seven percent difference, as Schwartz, Woloshin and Welch pointed out in the same journal.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11400
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:3lhrtm'
    '|a||One warning about the conversion. Fed an adjusted odds ratio, it gives biased estimates and wrong confidence intervals, so with a common outcome, estimate the risk ratio directly, with a log-binomial or a modified Poisson model.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 16680
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:45hg05'
    '|a||So with a common outcome, read an odds ratio as an exaggerated risk ratio, in whichever direction it points.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8400
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:17zqx2q'
    '|a||Next chapter: Time':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2320
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Risks and odds both count people. But in a real cohort, people are followed for different lengths of time.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8680
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Here are six patients followed after a first heart attack, an illustration. Three have a second one, after one, two and three years.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12240
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:8zhrgu'
    '|a||The others leave without it. The study ends, or they move away, and their follow-up is censored.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7600
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1u1dtxo'
    '|a||Three events among six people looks like a risk of fifty percent. But over what time? One patient was watched for a year, others for five.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10160
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:10u2h5h'
    '|a||The fix is to count time instead of people. Add up every year each patient was watched.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7040
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1xnc0fl'
    '|a||These six contribute twenty person-years between them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4200
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:13voku8'
    '|a||Three events in twenty person-years is a rate of zero point one five per person-year, or fifteen per hundred.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8560
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:sohazd'
    '|a||Two groups are compared by their rate ratio, which, like the risk ratio, is one when the exposure does nothing.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7760
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1rj65ga'
    '|a||Ten events in five hundred person-years against ten in two hundred and fifty is a rate ratio of one half, even though the counts are equal.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10480
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:2f09ck'
    '|a||With a constant rate, a rate turns back into a risk. After five years at zero point one five, the risk is one minus e to the minus zero point seven five, about fifty-three percent.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 15240
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1v8ab9v'
    '|a||Person-time has one assumption built in. The censored patients must be like those who stayed, because if the sickest drop out, the rate looks better than it is.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11800
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:19pfndn'
    '|a||Now imagine counting events per person-time in an ever thinner slice of time.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6280
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:hoa9ud'
    '|a||In the limit, the rate at a single instant is called the hazard.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5240
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1l5x8m'
    '|a||Shrink that slice of time to an instant, and the rate becomes the hazard, the event rate among those still event-free, right now.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10080
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Take patients with an advanced cancer, and say that on standard treatment they die at a constant hazard of zero point two a year, an illustration.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10920
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:llc4y1'
    '|a||Drawn as the share who have died, their curve rises as one minus e to the minus zero point two t.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9120
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1t03dr5'
    '|a||The hazard ratio compares two groups'' hazards at the same moment.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5000
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:qnm9l4'
    '|a||Now add a new drug, and start it at a hazard ratio of one, where its curve lies on top of the standard one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8000
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:10byjvc'
    '|a||Then lower the hazard ratio to one half. At every moment, patients on the new drug die at half the standard rate.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1guvvw2'
    '|a||This is the Cox proportional hazards model, from nineteen seventy-two. The baseline hazard is left unspecified, and because it cancels in the ratio, the hazard ratio needs no model of time at all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13920
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1tkmj70'
    '|a||Now the trap. After two years, thirty-three percent of the standard group have died, against eighteen percent on the new drug.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9120
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:51skq'
    '|a||The hazard ratio stays 0.5. Read the curves: what is the risk ratio of death at ten years?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7840
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:nfdt9v'
    '|a||After ten years it is eighty-six against sixty-three percent, a risk ratio of zero point seven three.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8520
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||The hazard ratio stayed at one half the whole time, yet the risk ratio drifted toward one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:mnego0'
    '|a||Everyone dies in the end. A drug that halves the hazard delays deaths, so the share who have died converges.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8560
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1yzaw41'
    '|a||Under proportional hazards, survival on the new drug is simply the standard group''s survival raised to the power of the hazard ratio.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1jh3www'
    '|a||Hernán added a subtler warning in twenty ten. Later hazards compare the survivors, and after the first deaths, the survivors in two arms are no longer alike, even in a randomized trial.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1ioqsf'
    '|a||The Cox model assumes the hazard ratio is the same at every moment. Often it is not.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7360
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Take a made-up comparison. A medical treatment leaves patients with a steady death rate of eight percent a year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6960
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1ioncz6'
    '|a||Surgery kills some patients early, around the operation, but leaves the survivors with a hazard of only three percent a year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:13m3hqu'
    '|a||In the first months the surgical hazard is far higher, so the hazard ratio is well above one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6760
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1mhrbl8'
    '|a||Later it settles near zero point four, and the survival curves cross, here at four years.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8080
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:o2s3fz'
    '|a||That breaks the proportional hazards assumption.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1ycz8h2'
    '|a||A single Cox hazard ratio would average an early harm and a late benefit, weighted by when the deaths happen, and describe neither.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10440
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:jyfvyz'
    '|a||To check the assumption, plot log minus log survival against log time and look for parallel lines, or test the Schoenfeld residuals for a trend.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11240
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1w2pgei'
    '|a||When it fails, report the curves themselves, hazard ratios for separate periods, or the restricted mean survival time, the area under each curve up to a fixed horizon.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12520
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:76kbuv'
    '|a||Up to four years, surgery loses area, about three tenths of a year of life on average.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6800
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1m4l8tg'
    '|a||After four years it gains it back, just over half a year.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5040
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1h65145'
    '|a||Over ten years, the net difference is about a fifth of a year, roughly ten weeks, in favour of surgery.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7120
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:148und0'
    '|a||That number is in time, not in ratios, and it stays meaningful whether hazards are proportional or not.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7800
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:ygic7m'
    '|a||Next chapter: Choosing a measure':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3080
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||One more property separates these measures, and it surprises experienced analysts. Take a trial with two equal strata, randomized within each, so there is no confounding.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13400
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Say the outcome is recovery from pneumonia within a week, with made-up numbers. Among older patients, recovery is fifty percent on placebo and eighty on treatment, an odds ratio of four.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13920
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:m7myyi'
    '|a||Among younger patients it is twenty against fifty percent, again an odds ratio of exactly four.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1kswlqc'
    '|a||Pool everyone, and recovery is thirty-five against sixty-five percent. The odds ratio is now three point four five.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9840
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:11kiiz0'
    '|a||So the pooled odds ratio is smaller than the odds ratio in every stratum, with no confounding at all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1kl1fqg'
    '|a||That is non-collapsibility. The risk ratio and the risk difference pool to values between the strata, but the odds ratio and the hazard ratio do not.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11360
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1u3xeqr'
    '|a||So adding a prognostic covariate to a logistic or a Cox model moves the estimate away from one, even in a perfectly randomized trial.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10240
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1agm5or'
    '|a||That change is not confounding. The conditional and the marginal odds ratios simply answer different questions.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8160
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:15parwz'
    '|a||Put together, each measure answers its own question, and only some designs can deliver it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7000
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:bfzme4'
    '|a||Which measures can a case-control study give you on its own? Sort each card.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5880
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1b76k5h'
    '|a||A case-control study gives you the odds ratio alone, unless the baseline risk is known from elsewhere, while a trial or a cohort can give you every one of them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12400
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||One habit protects readers from most of these traps. Give the absolute risks, at a stated time, next to every ratio.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9320
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:z6w9g9'
    '|a||One last check, on a new cancer trial, drawn the way we drew the hazards.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5520
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||In the control arm, eighty percent have died by ten years.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4640
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:junlwg'
    '|a||With a hazard ratio of 0.5 and proportional hazards, how many on the new drug have died by 10 years?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8360
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:3o0x6k'
    '|a||About fifty-five percent. Control survival is zero point two, and zero point two to the power of one half is zero point four five, so fifty-five percent have died: far from half of eighty.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 16440
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||On the curves, eighty percent dead in the control arm becomes about fifty-five on the new drug.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7520
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||Not forty. Halving the hazard slows the deaths; it does not halve them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6200
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:zk3z6i'
    '|a||So read every ratio as an answer to its own question, and ask for the absolute risks that say how many people it changes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9400
      voice: 'gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical #ctx:1enjwfb'
    '|a||Nine: ninety against ten. The odds run away as the risk nears one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6880
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||About fourteen: six hundred and forty-seven times twenty-seven, over six hundred and twenty-two times two.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8880
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||0 right on the first try.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2840
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||0 of 5 on the first try. A case-control study fixes how many cases and controls there are, so only odds survive.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10760
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||1 right on the first try.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4080
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||1 of 5 on the first try. A case-control study fixes how many cases and controls there are, so only odds survive.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9840
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||2 right on the first try.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3800
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||2 of 5 on the first try. A case-control study fixes how many cases and controls there are, so only odds survive.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10880
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||3 right on the first try.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4680
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||3 of 5 on the first try. A case-control study fixes how many cases and controls there are, so only odds survive.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11000
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||4 right on the first try.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3560
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||4 of 5 on the first try. A case-control study fixes how many cases and controls there are, so only odds survive.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10320
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||5 right on the first try.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3680
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||5 of 5 on the first try. A case-control study fixes how many cases and controls there are, so only odds survive.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10480
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
    '|a||A hazard ratio acts on the rate at each moment, not on the share who have died. Use survival, raised to the power of the hazard ratio.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10720
      voice: gemini:Charon | a calm, precise epidemiology lecturer; clear and unhurried, never theatrical
